  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 322 PM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery and RAP analysis currently depict broad \ntroughing across the eastern CONUS and ridging across the Desert \nSouthwest as a mid/upper shortwave treks toward the Northern \nPlains. At the surface, a tightening pressure gradient \ndeveloping in response to an approaching cold front has promoted\ngusty south winds across areas mainly west of Interstate 135. \nWith the front currently spanning from northern Minnesota to the\nNebraska Panhandle, we continue to expect the boundary to \ncontinue shifting southeast and touch off showers and storms \nacross northwest Kansas later this evening and overnight. A few \nof these may find their way into central Kansas early Tuesday \nmorning, though short-term models highlight better chances \nacross northern Kansas into southern Nebraska where greater \ninstability will reside. Highs in the afternoon will range from \nthe low 90s across central Kansas to the upper 90s along the \nKansas-Oklahoma border.\n\nAnother round of showers and storms is expected to develop later \nTuesday evening into the overnight hours as 700 mb waa develops in \nresponse to ripples in midlevel flow tracking across northern \nKansas. This will promote a little better coverage across central \nKansas (primarily along/north of US-56) late Tuesday night into \nWednesday morning. With the front continuing its shift southward, \nisolated to scattered rain chances will overspread most of the \nforecast area heading into Wednesday afternoon and evening. Moderate \ndeep-layer shear arising from strengthening winds aloft may allow \nfor strong to severe activity east of the Flint Hills during this \nperiod, though as the prior discussion pointed out, residual clouds \nfrom earlier activity may put a damper on instability that would \notherwise promote stronger storm development. Behind the front, \nafternoon highs across central Kansas are only forecast to climb \ninto the middle 80s, while locations ahead of the front \n(southern Kansas) will see another day of highs in the upper 90s.\n\nStorm chances will continue through Thursday into Thursday night, \nthough location will depend on how far south the effective front is \npushed by earlier activity. At this point, the greatest chances \nreside primarily south of US-56, though the northward extent of \nlikely storm chances may be closer to the Kansas-Oklahoma border if \nsolutions like the NAM/Euro pan out. Strong to marginally severe \nstorms will once again be possible during this period owing to \nstronger winds aloft. Additionally, with progged PWs around 2\", \nefficient rainfall may lead to localized flooding concerns. Thanks \nto cloud cover and the cooler airmass in place, temperatures \nThursday afternoon will range from the middle 80s across central \nKansas to the mid 90s across southeast Kansas.\n\nLooking ahead to the end of the week and beyond, long range models \nhighlight a mid/upper ridge building back into the Plains. This \nshould support dry and hot conditions returning to the area.",
